    weight={'key':'heilman-weight-floor','namespace':'probability.gaussian_noise_stability','logical_name':'heilman-positive-simplex-radial-weight-lower-bound','kind':'lemma','title':'Heilman positive simplex radial weight lower bound (153): J_+/sqrt(t)','statement':'Let S(b)=4 integral from 0 to infinity of u^2 exp(-u^2-2bu) du for real b, and H(q)=(pi/2) integral from 0 to 2/3 of v(1-3v/2)sin(pi v)S(-q cos(pi v))dv for q>=0. Let 0<rho<1, d=1-rho^2, alpha=rho/sqrt(d), kappa=rho^2/d, and j1=(9-pi sqrt(3))/(4pi^2). Define Jtilde(x)=alpha exp(-kappa x^2)[H(alpha x)+(j1/2)S(alpha x)] for x>=0. This is the positive simplex radial weight J_+(x^2)/x for x>0, with continuous extension at zero. For every x>=0, Jtilde(x)>=(6-pi)/(4pi^(3/2)) alpha exp(-kappa x^2)>0. This denominator lower bound alone implies no operator or Hilbert-Schmidt norm bound.','proof_text':'The integral defining S is positive. If b<=0, its integrand dominates the b=0 integrand, so S(b)>=sqrt(pi). For 0<=v<=1/2, -q cos(pi v)<=0. The integrand for H is nonnegative on the whole interval [0,2/3]; restrict it to [0,1/2] and use S>=sqrt(pi). Direct integration gives integral_0^(1/2) v(1-3v/2)sin(pi v)dv=(6-pi)/(2pi^3), hence H(q)>=(6-pi)/(4pi^(3/2)). Finally j1>0 and S(q)>0. Multiplication by alpha exp(-kappa x^2) proves the bound. This expands the primary source bound, not a new result.','assumptions':['0<rho<1','x>=0','S and H defined by the displayed convergent integrals','Primary-source bound (153); proof expansion unaudited'],'domain':'Gaussian noise stability','source':{'source_id':'arxiv:2608.00333v1:equation-153-positive-radial-weight','url':'https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.00333v1','revision':'v1; author code commit 6b720fe57bfe2a3c8fffc51521d00b31011aa1a3; read 2026-10-15','license':'arxiv-nonexclusive (mathematical fact restated and proof expanded in original words)','excerpt':None}}
